# Proof scripts NOT run in CI, and why.
#
# All of these were written against the LIVE project (koiplnmbgnqnbywhpjlf),
# where the catalogues, partner rows, service products, clinical staff and
# protocol rows they select as fixtures already exist. A fresh
# `supabase db reset` has only what the migrations plus supabase/seed/seed.sql
# create, which is a much smaller world: a script that starts with
# `select id into v_x from public.<catalogue> limit 1` and then raises when it
# finds nothing would fail the job for a missing fixture rather than for a
# real regression, and a wall of those teaches everyone to ignore the job.
#
# This is a backlog, not a verdict. The intended path is to move files up into
# ci.manifest a few at a time, each time confirming the script builds its own
# fixtures and passes against a clean `supabase db reset`. Do not add a file
# here to silence a genuine failure.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-05: none of the scripts below had ever been executed by any
# workflow. Nineteen were moved up to ci.manifest in that pass; what remains is
# still the same backlog, minus the three below, which are a different thing.
#
# ===========================================================================
# NOT A FIXTURE PROBLEM: three scripts here now FAIL for real. They stay
# excluded only because the behaviour they assert is genuinely absent, and
# closing each gap needs a migration, which is not this file's to write.
# Do not "fix" these by weakening the assertion.
#
#  1. broadcast_content_class_enforcement.sql -- public.admin_send_broadcast
#     does not consult private.broadcast_content_flags at all, and
#     notification_broadcasts carries no content CHECK (its only CHECK is
#     channels_nonempty). The heuristic itself is live and correct: it flags
#     "your diagnosis" and "your (test|lab|screening|biopsy) result". Nothing
#     reads it. A broadcast whose body says "Your diagnosis is confirmed,
#     please call the clinic" is accepted and sent to all_patients. The
#     server-side block this file was written to prove is not wired up.
#     Verified live 2026-09-05. Note this file previously aborted at 42501
#     before reaching that case -- it called private.* after switching to role
#     `authenticated`, which has no USAGE on schema private -- so it had never
#     run far enough to say so. That structural bug is fixed here; the finding
#     is what is left.
#
#  2. authenticated_table_grants.sql -- two RLS-enabled tables carry a policy
#     for `authenticated` and no privilege for the action that policy names,
#     so the policy governs nothing and PostgREST answers 42501, which the UI
#     renders as a confident empty result:
#       * public.sexual_health_privacy_settings has a SELECT policy for
#         authenticated and NO grant to authenticated at all (relacl is
#         postgres + service_role only), and no column-level grant either;
#       * public.provider_complaint_investigation_notes has an ALL policy for
#         authenticated but only INSERT/SELECT (relacl `ar`), so its UPDATE and
#         DELETE halves are unreachable. If that append-only shape is the
#         intent, the policy should say so rather than an ALL policy silently
#         half-working.
#     wearable_connections' UPDATE is NOT one of these: it is the deliberate
#     column-level narrowing that keeps access_token/refresh_token unreadable,
#     and the check accepts it. This file also used to abort with 22023 on the
#     DELETE probe (has_column_privilege has no DELETE privilege type), which
#     is why it had never reported any of this; that is fixed here.
#
#  3. actor_column_fk_documentation.sql -- public.finance_posting_failures
#     .resolved_by carries no column comment naming which table its FK points
#     at, the rule PR #485 established for exactly the 21 column names shared
#     between profiles and clinical_staff, where the wrong join returns NULL
#     silently. One column, and the fix is a COMMENT ON in a migration.
# ===========================================================================
#
# Found 2026-09-06 (PR #456), in neither list at all -- the original bug this
# file exists to stop, not a new instance of the two categories above. All
# three headers say "Run via npx supabase db query --linked", confirming they
# were written against the live project's own fixtures, not a bare
# `supabase db reset`:
#
#  4. abnormal_screening_result_upgrade.sql -- the live proof for this
#     platform's single highest-priority business event (CLAUDE.md: "Abnormal
#     result -> Category 1 upgrade ... never lose it, never let it fail
#     silently"). Two separate reasons it cannot move to ci.manifest as-is:
#     (a) it is a fixture-backlog case like the plain list below --
#     `select ... from public.profiles where role = 'patient' limit 1` with no
#     null guard, and a fresh `supabase db reset` seeds zero patient profiles
#     (supabase/seed/seed.sql: "Populates the global reference catalogues
#     only -- no tenant/patient data"), so v_org/v_patient come back NULL and
#     every insert after it errors on a NOT NULL violation; and (b) even once
#     given a fixture, the file has no PASS/FAIL verdict mechanism at all --
#     it prints a `probe` table for a human to compare against a documented
#     "Expect:" comment, never raises, never prints a FAIL in a verdict
#     column. run-db-proofs.sh only fails a script on a raised exception or an
#     `... | FAIL` trailing column, so as written this would report a false
#     PASS regardless of what the trigger actually does -- exactly the
#     "vacuous test" failure mode the whole apparatus exists to catch, on the
#     one proof that can least afford it. Promoting this needs both a
#     self-built fixture (insert its own org+patient, the way
#     payer_board_outcomes_report.sql does) AND a real verdict block
#     (`case when ... then 'PASS' else 'FAIL' end` per case, plus a closing
#     `raise exception` on any FAIL) before it goes anywhere near ci.manifest.
#
#  5. payer_board_outcomes_report.sql -- has a real, correct PASS/FAIL verdict
#     block and a closing `raise exception` on any FAIL (not a vacuous test),
#     but is a genuine fixture-backlog case: it needs an existing
#     `public.organisations` row (likely fine -- the default consumer org is
#     migration-created) AND an existing active `role = 'admin'` profile
#     (`raise exception 'no active admin profile'` otherwise), and a fresh
#     reset creates neither people nor admins, only catalogue rows. Building
#     its own admin fixture (insert into auth.users + profiles, the way this
#     file already does for its patient/payer fixtures) would let it move up.
#
#  6. support_centre.sql -- same shape as #5, but a much higher bar: its
#     own fixture-resolution query requires ALL of a Care Coordinator, a
#     non-coordinator clinical-tier staff member, a Clinical Director, an
#     admin, and 2+ patients already present in the SAME organisation
#     (`raise exception 'need an org with 2+ patients, a Care Coordinator...'`
#     otherwise) -- exactly the class of fixture this file's own header
#     describes, at the more demanding end of it.
# ===========================================================================
#
# Found 2026-09-06 (result-recall-reconciliation), same fixture-backlog
# category as #4-6 above -- selects an existing fixture rather than building
# its own, so it cannot run against a fresh `supabase db reset`:
#
#  7. result_release_policies.sql -- the live proof for the
#     result_release_policies RLS gate (20260829135012_result_release_policies.sql).
#     Its DO block starts `select id, organisation_id into v_patient, v_org
#     from public.profiles where role = 'patient' limit 1` and raises
#     immediately if none is found; a fresh reset seeds only catalogue rows,
#     no tenant/patient data (same seed.sql note as #4-6), so this always
#     raises rather than running. Unlike #4, it DOES carry a real per-case
#     PASS/FAIL verdict (five cases, each a `case when ... then 'PASS' else
#     'FAIL' end`) -- it just has no fixture of its own yet. Building one
#     (insert its own org + patient + clinician profile, the way
#     payer_board_outcomes_report.sql does) would let it move up.
# ===========================================================================
abnormal_screening_result_upgrade.sql
